    "case_id": "737",
    "case_summary": "A 40-year-old woman, G4P1, presented with pain in the right lower abdomen persisting for eight years, with recent aggravation and abdominal expansion sensation in the past month. Surgical history includes: cesarean section (2009), laparoscopic myomectomy (2012), surgeries for fibroids and ovarian cysts (2015, 2018), hysteroscopic IUD placement (2018), endometriomas puncture (2019), and total abdominal hysterectomy with right adnexectomy and left salpingectomy (2020). The pain gradually increased requiring oral painkillers. Patient has a two-year smoking history (7 cigarettes/day) but quit 5 years ago.\n\nPatient appeared in visible pain with forced posture. Abdomen was soft and tender in lower part, without rebound tenderness. Pelvic examination revealed: no hymen/vaginal obstruction, smooth sutured end. A 10 cm mass was palpable in pelvic cavity, fixed to anterior rectum, with clear boundary, hard texture, significant tenderness and limited mobility. Rectal examination showed smooth mucosa with a hard, mobile, non-tender mass 7 cm from anus.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:  - CA125: 35.08 U/ml  - Hormone levels (post-surgery): LH 44.87 IU/L, FSH 108.8 IU/L, Prolactin 6.62 μg/L, Progesterone 0.16 ng/mL, Estradiol <15 pg/ml, Testosterone 12.08 ng/dL- Imaging Studies:  - MRI and CT findings: Irregular mass in pelvic area with multiple nodules  - Image Description: MRI showed multiple soft tissue shadows with uneven signals, blurred margin with adjacent rectum and sigmoid colon  - CT Description: Irregular lobular mass shadows behind bladder with uneven intensity and cystic regions, inhomogeneous enhancement with local ring enhancement- Pathological Findings:  - Biopsy results: Smooth muscle tumor  - Immunohistochemistry: Positive for CD34, SMA, desmin, focal CD10, ER, PR, and 2% Ki-67; Negative for CD117, S-100, and Dog-1",
    "frontier": [
      "B1",
      "B2",
      "B3"
    ],
